\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:relatedwork}

\textbf{Automated specification/summary synthesis.} The automated synthesis of specifications/summaries is particularly challenging, especially with existence of loops. Traditional techniques can be   classified into two categories: dynamic analysis and static analysis. Dynamic analysis infers specifications and invariants by leveraging runtime data obtained from test cases. Daikon~\cite{daikon} collects runtime data by executing test units and generates a candidate set of invariants based on a library of invariant  templates.
%, then filters out invalid invariants using the collected data. 
Sharma \emph{et al}.~\cite{A_Data_Driven_Approach_for_Algebraic_Loop} 
%build a data matrix from runtime data, compute the null space of this matrix to obtain candidate invariants,
adopts a similar dynamic analysis  approach but furthermore checks soundness of the candidate invariants through SMT solvers. DIG~\cite{dig} integrates concepts from linear algebra, geometry, and formal methods to infer invariants directly from observed program traces without need to filter out predefined templates. 
%It also employs filtering techniques to exclude spurious invariants. 
 However, the approach based on dynamic analysis heavily depends on the quality of the test units, and an excessive number of templates can also lead to a decrease in performance.

%textbf{Static analysis.} 
Static analysis eliminates the dependency on test cases and explores a variety of techniques, including 
%black-box learning~\cite{ice}, 
abstract interpretation~\cite{Abstract_Interpretation_A_Unified_Lattice_Model_for_Static_Analysis_of_Programs_by_Construction_or_Approximation_of_Fixpoints}~\cite{Automatic_Discovery_of_Linear_Restreints_among_Variables_of_a_Program}, liquid type inference~\cite{Refinement_Types_for_Haskell}~\cite{Refinement_Type_Inference_via_Horn_Constraint_Optimization}, abductive inference~\cite{Inductive_Invariant_Generation_via_Abductive_Inference}, predicate abstraction~\cite{Deriving_Invariants_by_Algorithmic_Learning_Decision_Procedures_and_Predicate_Abstraction}, interpolant~\cite{FiB_Squeezing_loop,Nonlinear_Craig_Interpolant_Generation}, symbolic execution~\cite{Backward_Symbolic_Execution_with_Loop_Folding}~\cite{SymInfer_Inferring_Numerical_Invariants_using_Symbolic_States}, and so on. 
%Garg \emph{et al}.~\cite{ice} introduces a robust black-box learning framework, ICE, which incorporates "implications" as crucial feedback for synthesizing program invariants, and designs strongly convergent black-box learning algorithms for numerical and quantified invariants.
Cousot \emph{et al}.~\cite{Automatic_Discovery_of_Linear_Restreints_among_Variables_of_a_Program} utilizes convex polyhedra along with transformations and widening operations to discover linear invariants based on abstract interpretation. Hashimoto \emph{et al}.~\cite{Refinement_Type_Inference_via_Horn_Constraint_Optimization} generate refinement types encoding maximally-preferred invariants  by reducing type inference to an %existentially-quantified Horn clause 
optimization problem.
%and solving it with a template-based iterative improvement algorithm. 
Jung \emph{et al}.~\cite{Deriving_Invariants_by_Algorithmic_Learning_Decision_Procedures_and_Predicate_Abstraction} use the CDNF exact learning algorithm~\cite{Exact_learning_boolean_functions_via_the_monotone_theory} with teacher-provided queries for  invariant inference.
%, though this requires manually-provided atomic predicates.
Lin \emph{et al}.~\cite{FiB_Squeezing_loop} synthesize inductive invariants using Craig interpolants derived from reachability analyses, and nonlinear  Craig interpolant generation was first studied in~\cite{Nonlinear_Craig_Interpolant_Generation}.
%, but cannot handle non-linear arithmetic or complex data structures. %Amrollahi \emph{et al}.~\cite{Solving_Invariant_Generation_for_Unsolvable_Loops} identifies defective variables by analyzing variable dependencies and employs a constraint solver to synthesize polynomial invariants associated with defective variables. However, it is limited to affine loops. 
%Our proposed approach LLM combined with symbolic execution to generate a wide variety of invariants as broadly as possible. This hybrid methodology aims to overcome some of the limitations faced by traditional methods, such as dependency on initial predicates, inability to handle complex data types, and restrictions to specific loop classes.
%\textbf{Symbolic Execution.}
%As a form of static analysis, symbolic execution records the state at each program point, providing rich information for specification generation. To address the challenges symbolic execution faces with loops, numerous techniques have been proposed and integrated into symbolic execution frameworks. 
Chalupa and Strejcek~\cite{Backward_Symbolic_Execution_with_Loop_Folding} propose an extension of backward symbolic execution (BSE) called loop folding  to derive loop invariants  that are sufficient to prove the unreachability of error locations~\cite{Snugglebug_a_Powerful_Approach_to_Weakest_Preconditions}. %which overcomes the non-termination issues encountered by standard BSE when dealing with loops.  
SymInfer~\cite{SymInfer_Inferring_Numerical_Invariants_using_Symbolic_States}  infers program invariants from the symbolic states generated by symbolic execution of Java programs.
Wu \emph{et al}.~\cite{Synthesizing_Invariants_for_Polynomial_Programs_by_Semidefinite_Programming} solve the strong and weak invariant synthesis problems of polynomial programs by combining invariant templates, semidefinite programming and constraint solving techniques. 
 %goes beyond traditional symbolic execution by not only directly deriving numerical invariants such as inequalities inferred via SMT optimization but also by validating and refining candidate invariants initially extracted from concrete program traces. 
 %
 %In contrast, our method combines symbolic execution with LLMs to promote the efficiency of loop invariant generation.  
 %We extract symbolic states upon first entering a loop through symbolic execution, and then leverage the generative capabilities of LLMs to infer potential invariants.

\textbf{Specification/summary synthesis with machine learning.}
Recent advancements in machine learning~\cite{iRank, Lemur, SpecGen, Code2Inv_a_Deep_Learning_Framework_for_Program_Verification, Loop_Invariant_Inference_through_SMT_Solving_Enhanced_Reinforcement_Learning, LLM_Generated_Invariants_for_Bounded_Model_Checking_Without_Loop_Unrolling}, particularly deep learning and reinforcement learning, have spurred new approaches for invariant and specification generation. CODE2INV~\cite{Code2Inv_a_Deep_Learning_Framework_for_Program_Verification}, based on Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS)~\cite{Counterexample_Guided_Inductive_Synthesis_Modulo_Theories}, utilizes graph neural networks  to learn neural representations of program structures. 
%It frames loop invariant generation as a deep reinforcement learning problem, where a neural policy is automatically learned through real-time interaction with a given verifier. 
While CODE2INV employs an end-to-end reinforcement learning approach, it often faces challenges with limited performance and sparse rewards. To address these issues, LIPuS~\cite{Loop_Invariant_Inference_through_SMT_Solving_Enhanced_Reinforcement_Learning} establishes a clearer division of labor between the capabilities of RL and SMT solvers and introduces a novel two-dimensional reward mechanism
%, which provides feedback that is more fine-grained and instructive than that of CODE2INV, thereby effectively 
to alleviate the sparse reward problem of CODE2INV. However, training model requires significant computational power and large datasets. 
%The  difficulty in collecting such datasets leads to  substantial human and material resources.

\textbf{Specification/summary synthesis with LLM.}
%With the development of LLMs, their increasingly powerful logical analysis capabilities are leading more and more people to explore the possibility of using LLMs to automatically synthesize specifications and invariants. 
Pei \emph{et al}. \cite{Can_Large_Language_Models_Reason_About_Program_Invariants} demonstrates that fine-tuning pre-trained LLMs on code can effectively predict program invariants, highlighting their potential in this field. ACInv~\cite{Enhancing_Automated_Loop_Invariant_Generation_for_Complex_Programs_with_Large_Language_Models} utilizes static analysis to extract essential information about loops and data structures, which is then embedded into prompts for an LLM to generate candidate invariants and abstract predicates for data structures. 
%Subsequently, an LLM-based evaluator assesses these generated invariants and refines them to produce enhanced, correct invariants. 
Loopy~\cite{Finding_Inductive_Loop_Invariants_using_Large_Language_Models} uses Houdini algorithm to efficiently find a valid subset of inductive invariants from multiple LLM-generated candidate sets. AutoSpec~\cite{autospec} decomposes programs into smaller parts and uses carefully designed prompts including few-shot examples, to guide an LLM in generating specifications and verify them using Frama-C.

\textbf{Abstract summary generation.}
Synthesizing easily understandable abstract specifications or summaries for functions  has also been studied. AMURTH~\cite{Synthesizing_Abstract_Transformers} starts from user-provided concrete semantics of operations, and employs a dual Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS) approach to 
generate abstract transformers expressed within a DSL in 
abstract domain. 
%It employs a dual Counterexample-Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS) approach—two interleaved loops that check for soundness and precision, generating positive and negative counterexamples, respectively—to refine candidate transformers and thereby generate abstract transformers. 
%Building upon this, 
SPYRO~\cite{Synthesizing_Specifications} expands the problem domain from specific  abstract transformers to more general specification synthesis, and enhances efficiency and quality through algorithmic optimization.
%such as inner and outer search loops, more fine-grained negative example handling, and a modified CHECKPRECISION. 
%Drawing inspiration from these tools, we utilize a custom DSL and employ heuristic search to synthesize our desired summaries.

Unlike existing approaches, our method uniquely integrates symbolic execution, LLMs, and formal verification in a unified framework to efficiently synthesize function summaries at multiple abstraction levels.



\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this paper, we have presented a novel, end-to-end framework for automatically synthesizing multi-granularity function summaries for C programs by tightly integrating path-sensitive symbolic execution, large language models, and formal verification. Our approach generates formally-verified relatively strongest postconditions (RSPs), infers precise loop invariants via template-guided LLM synthesis and iterative refinement, and automatically abstracts these RSPs into domain-specific summaries. Experimental results on standard benchmarks and real-world aerospace code show that our tool outperforms existing methods in both precision and efficiency, achieving strong results on invariant generation, particularly on SyGuS numeric single-program benchmarks, and  summarizing complex legacy functions.

For future work, we will extend our approach to handle recursive functions and recursive data structures,  and devlelop richer domain-specific languages that incorporate user-defined abstract predicates. We also plan to improve our invariant generation methods to deal with these complex cases through LLM fine-tuning and specialized training procedures.

