\begin{theorem}[\cite{fullproofs}]
\label{thm: special annihilator precise formulation}
Let $c$ be an integral configuration and $f$ a non-trivial integral annihilator of $c$.
If $r$ is a dilation constant of $c$ with respect to $f$, then for all $\bu \in \supp(f)$ the configuration $c$ is annihilated by the polynomial
$$
\prod_{\bv \in \supp(f) \setminus \{\bu\}} (X^{r(\bv-\bu)}-1).
$$ 
\end{theorem}

\noindent
Note that the polynomial in the above theorem depends only on $f$ and the dilation constant $r$.
Thus, if a family of configurations have a common annihilator $f$ and a common dilation constant with respect to $f$, then they are all annihilated by the polynomial
$$
