$$
where $k$ is a non-negative integer --- the integer part of $\frac{T}{\sqrt{d}R}$ --- and $q < 2R$ is a non-negative real number.
Clearly, $C$ contains at least $k^d$ separate hypercubes with sides of length $2R$ and hence at least $k^d$ separate balls of radius $R$.
Since $R$ is the covering radius of $S$, each ball of radius $R$ contains at least one point of $S$.
Thus,
