    If the product $(X^{\bv_1}-1) \cdots (X^{\bv_m}-1)$ of difference $\R^d$-polynomials annihilates $c$, 
    then there exist functions $c_1, \ldots, c_m \in \Z^{\R^d}$ such that $(X^{\bv_i}-1) c_i = 0$ for each $i$ and 
    $$
    c = c_1 + \ldots + c_m.
    $$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    % Proof is by induction on $m$ using Lemma \ref{genlemma1} just like in \cite{fullproofs}.
    The proof is by induction on $m$.
    For $m=1$ the claim is clear.
    Assume then that $m \geq 2$ and that the claim holds for $m-1$.
    Since $(X^{\bv_m}-1) c$ is annihilated by $(X^{\bv_1}-1)\cdots (X^{\bv_{m-1}}-1)$, by the induction hypothesis there exist functions $c'_1, \ldots , c'_{m-1} \in \C^{\R^d}$ such that 
    $$
    (X^{\bv_m}-1) c = 
    c'_1 + \ldots + c'_{m-1}
    $$
    and each $c'_i$ is annihilated by $X^{\bv_i}-1$.
    By Lemma \ref{genlemma1} for each $i \in \{1,\ldots,m-1 \}$ there exists a function $c_i$ such that $(X^{\bv_m}-1) c_i = c'_i$ and $(X^{\bv_i}-1)c_i=0$.
    Set $c_m = c - c_1 - \ldots - c_{m-1}$.
    Then clearly $c = c_1+\ldots+c_m$ and moreover
    \begin{align*}
    (X^{\bv_m}-1) c_m &= (X^{\bv_m}-1) (c -c_1 - \ldots -c_{m-1}) \\
    & = (X^{\bv_m}-1) c - (X^{\bv_m}-1)c_1 - \ldots - (X^{\bv_m}-1)c_{m-1} \\
    & = c'_1 + \ldots + c'_{m-1} - c'_1 - \ldots - c'_{m-1} \\ &= 0.
    \end{align*}
    Hence, $c=c_1+\ldots+c_m$ and each $c_i$ is annihilated by $(X^{\bv_i}-1)$.
    The claim follows.
\end{proof}

