\begin{corollary}
  Let $c \in \A^{\R}$ be a 1-dimensional Delone configuration with a non-trivial annihilator.
  Then $c$ is a Meyer configuration.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
  By Theorem \ref{thm:1d delone with annihilators is periodic} the Delone configuration $c$ is periodic and hence by Lemma \ref{lemma:strongly periodic delone sets are meyer} it is a Meyer configuration.
\end{proof}

For $d\geq 2$, there exist Delone configurations with non-trivial annihilators that are not Meyer configurations.
% Thus, the above corollary does not hold in higher dimensions.
For example, consider a Delone set which is periodic but not strongly periodic and not a Meyer set.
Its indicator function is a Delone configuration that has a non-trivial annihilator, and it is not a Meyer configuration.
Thus, the above corollary does not hold in higher dimensions.
