Recall that a \textit{\textbf{pre-filter}} $F$ on a pre-ordered set $(P,\leq)$ is a subset of $P$ satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item $F$ is non-empty.
	\item $F$ is downward directed, that is, for every $a,b\in F$, there is some $c\in F$ such that $c\leq a,b$.
	\item $F$ is an upper set or upward closed, that is, for every $a\in F$ and $b\in P$, if $a\leq b$ then $b\in F$.
\end{enumerate} The smallest pre-filter containing an element $a$ is a \textit{\textbf{principal pre-filter}} and $a$ is a \textit{\textbf{principal element}} --- it is given by
\begin{align*} 
	\uparrow_{(P,\leq)} a:=\{b\in P\mid a\leq b\}.
\end{align*}

The following observation motivates our notation using the symbol $\uparrow$:

\begin{fact} The set $\uparrow_{ \mathfrak{(A,B)}} t$ is the principal pre-filter with respect to the generalization ordering $\sqsubseteq_{ \mathfrak{(A,B)}}$ generated by $t$.
\end{fact}

