        Consider the scenario sketched in~\cref{fig:placement}, where one atom in the entanglement zone is reused from the previous \mbox{two-qubit} gate layer, and three other atoms initially located in the storage zone will be moved to the entanglement zone.
        Mainly optimizing for accumulated movement distance yields the placement as shown in~\cref{fig:placement:bad}.
        While atoms are indeed close, this placement prohibits the parallel movement of all three atoms indicated by the grey arrows as this would violate the non-crossing constraint (cf.~\cref{sec:zoned architecture}).
