\subsection{Multi-core versus FPGA}
Previous work has shown that multi-core simulators offer faster compilation, whereas FPGAs deliver higher simulation speed.
Following the experimental setup in~\cite{elsabbagh2023accelerating}, we benchmark CCSS on Chronos~\cite{abeydeera2020chronos}, a 128-PE graph accelerator.

The CPU baseline uses Verilator, while the FPGA baseline employs two Alveo U250 boards interconnected via a 200 Gbps link.
However, inter-board routing significantly extends the critical path, limiting the FPGA clock frequency to just a few MHz—even though single 7 nm parts typically achieve 100–600 MHz~\cite{intel_agilex_video_wp}.

Thanks to its partition-aware scheduler, CCSS avoids this bottleneck and outperforms the FPGA in simulation speed.
Moreover, it compiles Chronos in just 8 minutes, enabling nearly 57 CCSS simulation runs within the time required for 1-billion-cycle FPGA emulation, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:comparison}.


\section{Design overview}
\label{sec:overview}



As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:overview}, we propose CCSS, an accelerator for RTL simulation.
CCSS is a LUT-based multi-core architecture. Through the co-design of the compiler, computing cores, and NoC, we achieve acceleration in both combinational logic computation and sequential logic synchronization.

\begin{figure}[tb] 
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{figure/figure3.pdf}
    \caption{The overview design of CCSS, which is a LUT-based multi-core architecture. Our compiler’s design philosophy is time-division multiplexing of LUT units.}
    \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Fast combinational logic computing}
\textbf{Compiler Design}: Our frontend compilation strategy resembles that of FPGAs, utilizing a LUT-based netlist as an intermediate representation to emulate arbitrary combinational logic. The netlist comprises three main components: LUTs, DFFs, and Block RAM (BRAM), mapped respectively onto our compute array, SRAM, and hardware BRAM. Unlike traditional FPGA backends that directly map LUT nodes one-to-one to computation units, we apply time-division multiplexing, assigning multiple LUT nodes to a single unit across several cycles. Nevertheless, CCSS achieves higher simulation speeds compared to FPGA-based methods due to its increased simulation frequency. A key challenge in our compilation approach—DAG partitioning—is addressed in Section~\ref{sec:dag}.

\textbf{Computing Core Design}: Each CCSS computing core consists of LUTs array and SRAM. Unlike FPGAs, which rely on interconnect wires, CCSS uses a memory-access-based communication model where LUTs exchange data through shared SRAM. This design eliminates complex wiring and long critical paths, allowing for higher operating frequencies. To hide memory access latency and improve simulation speed, we implement a compute-memory overlap strategy within each core, detailed in Section~\ref{sec:core}.

\subsection{Fast sequential logic synchronization}
\textbf{NoC Design}: After each CCSS core simulates one RTL cycle, synchronization is performed via a hierarchical NoC. Cores are grouped into clusters with low-latency crossbar interconnects for intra-cluster communication, while inter-cluster communication uses a ring topology. This two-level design suits the system's needs: most synchronization occurs within clusters, and the ring’s low-latency links are sufficient for the lower-throughput inter-cluster traffic. Details are provided in Section~\ref{sec:noc}.


