\section{Balanced DAG Partition}
\label{sec:dag}

\subsection{Motivation}
RTL DAGs effectively capture the logical flow of simulations and naturally contain many independent sub-streams, making them well-suited for parallel acceleration~\cite{qian2011accelerating}. Prior approaches, like Verilator Multi-thread~\cite{verilator2020} and Sphinx~\cite{pu2023sphinx}, partition the DAG into subgraphs for multi-core execution. However, they often overlook data dependencies, resulting in limited speedup and poor core utilization. To address this, we introduce a fiber-based DAG partitioning method that improves parallel efficiency across cores.

\subsection{Challenge and Insight}
Partitioning DAGs into fibers for parallel acceleration presents two primary challenges:

\begin{enumerate} 
\item \textbf{Load imbalance between cores:} To reduce inter-core communication, prior works (e.g., RepCut~\cite{wang2023repcut}, Manticore~\cite{emami2023manticore}, Parendi~\cite{emami2024parendi}) partition DAGs into independent fibers. We follow this approach but schedule at the LUT node level rather than arithmetic operations. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:dag_partition}, traditional DAGs treat operations like addition and multiplication equally, whereas in our framework, they differ greatly in LUT counts—often by an order of magnitude. Naive assignments (e.g., merging fiber1 and fiber2 in core0 and assigning fiber3 to core1) can cause significant load imbalance. 
\item \textbf{Redundant computation:} Fibers naturally form tree-like structures, often leading to overlapping nodes between adjacent fibers (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_partition}(b)). If fiber1 and fiber2 are mapped to different cores, up to 64 LUT nodes may be redundantly computed (Figure~\ref{fig:dag_partition}(c)), degrading simulation performance. \end{enumerate}

We make two key observations: (1) Fiber granularity is often too coarse and can be refined through further partitioning and merging to improve load balance; (2) Load imbalance and redundant computation are not strictly correlated, requiring a multi-objective optimization. Based on these insights, we propose an efficient fiber-partitioning algorithm that jointly optimizes load balance and redundancy.

\subsection{Approach}
Our method for minimizing computational redundancy while maintaining load balance across cores consists of two steps:



\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Determining the required number of cores:} During synthesis, we compute the total number of LUT nodes and divide it by the target core count to derive the ideal per-core load, denoted as \( N_{\text{balance}} \). Fibers exceeding this threshold are split by incrementally selecting register vector bits until a sub-fiber exceeds \( N_{\text{balance}} \); the previous bit is then chosen as the split point.
For example, in Figure~\ref{fig:dag_partition}(c), fiber3 is divided into two sub-fibers with 400 and 500 LUTs, respectively, achieving balanced core utilization.

\item \textbf{Fiber merging heuristic:}
To optimize fiber assignments for both load balance and minimal redundancy, we use a multi-start hill climbing algorithm~\cite{tsamardinos2006max}.
Starting from a random assignment, we define a cost function that considers both imbalance and redundancy.
Fibers are iteratively moved between groups: beneficial moves are kept, others reverted.
The process continues until no further improvement or a maximum iteration count is reached.
Multiple restarts help avoid local optima and yield a near-optimal fiber assignment.
\end{enumerate}

Let \( \mathcal{H} = \{H_1, H_2, \dots, H_k\} \) be the merged fiber set, where \( N \) is the number of LUTs in the original netlist, and \( \alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R} \) are user-defined parameters.
Minimize the following objective:
\[
\text{minimize}\quad \alpha \cdot \text{Extra}(\mathcal{H}) + \beta \cdot \text{Imbalance}(\mathcal{H})
\]
Compute extra computation overhead (\(\text{Extra}\)) and load imbalance (\(\text{Imbalance}\)):
\[
\text{Extra}(\mathcal{H}) = \left|\sum_{i=1}^{k}|H_i| - |N|\right|
\]
\[
\text{Imbalance}(\mathcal{H}) = \sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{k}\left(|H_i| - \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{k}|H_j|}{k}\right)^2}
\]

We demonstrate the effectiveness of our partitioning algorithm using the JPEG benchmark~\cite{ultraembedded2023jpegdecoder} as a case study. Compared to directly applying hill climbing with individual registers as fiber root nodes, our method preserves register vector semantics in the RTL and performs iterations using register vectors as roots. As a result, under the same number of iterations, it reduces the Extra metric by 26.8× and the Imbalance metric by 30.8×, highlighting the importance of a good initial solution.

\section{Efficient In-Core Parallel Computation}
\label{sec:core}

\subsection{Motivation}
The previous section focused on fiber-based partitioning to enhance parallelism across cores.
However, parallel efficiency within each core is equally critical: poor in-core scheduling that causes frequent LUT stalls can significantly increase computation time—potentially reducing simulation speed by half if excessive idle cycles occur.

This section therefore addresses the problem of optimizing parallel execution within individual compute cores.

\subsection{Challenge and Insight}
To improve in-core computation efficiency, we face two major challenges:

\textbf{Computation Resource Allocation}: Each fiber assigned to a core is a LUT-based computation graph comprising thousands of nodes—far exceeding the number of available physical LUT units.
The complex data dependencies within each fiber pose significant challenges for efficient time-multiplexing of these nodes onto a limited set of LUT resources, while maintaining high hardware utilization.

\textbf{Memory Access Design}: While fiber-based partition removes inter-core communication, intra-core LUT nodes still exchange data via SRAM-based shared memory. Due to LUTs’ 4-input, 1-output structure, SRAM demands many read ports, causing asymmetric bandwidth needs. Typical single- or dual-port SRAMs~\cite{noguchi2008best} can’t meet this. For instance, 4 LUT units require 16 read ports; using dual-port SRAMs means at least 8 SRAM blocks, increasing area and leaving many write ports underused.

This section identifies layered topological sorting as key to tackling these challenges. Based on this, we develop an efficient fiber-to-LUT mapping and optimize each core’s memory architecture, boosting parallel execution efficiency of multiple LUTs within a core.

\begin{figure}[h]  
    \centering  
        \includegraphics[width=0.40\textwidth]{figure/figure5.pdf}  
    \caption{Layered topological sorting exposes two independent layers; nodes in layers 0 and 1 can run in parallel. The core’s five LUT units bound the number of nodes executed per cycle.} 
    \label{fig:layersorting} 
\end{figure} 

\subsection{Approach}
\subsubsection{Computation resource allocation}

To address the challenge of computation resource allocation, an effective scheduling strategy is required to map LUT nodes in a DAG onto the available LUT computation units.
We apply layered topological sorting to the LUT-DAG, placing each node in the first layer whose predecessors are already assigned. Nodes in the same layer are independent. A sliding-window schedule, whose width equals the number of LUT units per core, is used. For example, in Figure~\ref{fig:layersorting}, the core with five units processes nodes 0–4 in cycle 1, 5–9 in cycle 2, and so on.

\begin{figure}[h]  
    \centering  
        \includegraphics[width=0.40\textwidth]{figure/figure6.pdf}  
    \caption{In our implementation, we use four 5R1W SRAMs, providing a total of 20 ports, which can be connected to five 4-input LUTs.} 
    \label{fig:sram} 
\end{figure} 

\subsubsection{Memory Access Design}

To support high-speed access, we design an asymmetric multi-port SRAM matching the 4-input, 1-output nature of LUTs, which requires high read bandwidth. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:sram}, data is accessed via an address port with fixed-width words (e.g.8, 16, or 32 bits). Since adding read ports can reduce memory frequency~\cite{wang2014low, hsiao2014design, ohara20231w}, we use multiple SRAM blocks to balance read bandwidth and access speed.

Specifically, we adopt a 5R1W SRAM~\cite{hsiao2014design}, providing five read ports and one write port per block, supporting 1.5GHz access with 32-bit data words. To enable fine-grained access, a multiplexer selects specific bits from each word, allowing efficient single-bit reads required by computations.



\section{Low-latency NoC Design}
\label{sec:noc}


\subsection{Motivation}
Each RTL simulation cycle includes two phases: combinational logic computation and sequential logic synchronization. The previous sections optimize computation, enabling each core to process its DAG subgraph efficiently. However, before proceeding to the next cycle, cores must synchronize to ensure consistent signal sharing. This synchronization often accounts for 20–50\% of total cycle time~\cite{emami2023manticore}, making it a key performance bottleneck. Reducing this latency is critical for faster RTL simulation.


\subsection{Challenge and Insight}
Synchronization depends on inter-core interconnects for any-to-any data transfer and involves two key steps: injecting results from SRAM into the NoC and transmitting them to other cores. Each step presents its own challenges.

\textbf{Sparse data injection challenge}: Simulation results are sparsely scattered in SRAM, making them hard to read, pack, and inject into the NoC efficiently. Due to complex sub-DAG dependencies, register vector values are stored as individual bits across memory, rather than in contiguous locations, complicating data extraction for communication.
\textbf{Physical constraints challenge}: In large-scale NoCs connecting thousands of cores,
maintaining low latency under area and power constraints is difficult. Topologies like mesh and ring support local communication but require many routers, increasing hop counts and delays~\cite{abeyratne2013scaling}. To address this, we introduce two techniques:
\textbf{1)} Reusing memory access circuitry as the interface between compute cores and the NoC, activated post-computation to mitigate sparse data injection;
\textbf{2)} Performing compile-time analysis to detect inter-core synchronization patterns and group strongly dependent cores into clusters, where a high-speed crossbar enables fast synchronization.

Combining interface reuse with communication-aware clustering significantly reduces synchronization latency and improves overall RTL simulation performance.

\subsection{Approach}
\subsubsection{Reusing Memory Access Circuits to Output Reg Vectors to the NoC}

To address the sparse distribution of simulation results in SRAM, we propose reusing idle memory access circuitry after computation. This circuit efficiently locates scattered bits in SRAM, enabling retrieval of an entire register vector in one cycle. For local synchronization, data is written directly via low-latency local paths, while remote synchronization relies on the NoC, incurring higher latency.

\subsubsection{Synchronization with Remote Cores}

For synchronization between remote and local cores, we first synchronize remote cores via the NoC. This allows the NoC to overlap local core synchronization during packet transmission, hiding local synchronization delay. The differing synchronization needs across cores can be modeled as a graph partitioning problem: dividing M cores into N clusters to maximize intra-cluster communication. Efficient algorithms like METIS~\cite{karypis_metis} can solve this and guide NoC design, enabling low-latency synchronization within clusters and fewer hops between them. Using a cycle-accurate NoC simulator, we found synchronization takes 50.8\% of total mesh network time, while design area is reduced to 18.6\% of the original.





\section{Evaluation}
\label{sec:exp}


\subsection{Experimental Setup}
The RTL simulation time cost of CCSS is measured using the following tools.
\textbf{Architecture Simulator}: We developed a cycle-accurate simulator with two components—the computing core and the NoC—defining computation and data transfer interfaces. It measures computation and synchronization cycles per RTL cycle.
\textbf{CAD Tools}: The hardware is designed in Verilog and synthesized using Synopsys Design Compiler with the TSMC 28nm standard cell library. The critical path delay of the slowest logic module is 0.53 ns, including setup and hold times. However, the overall operating frequency is ultimately constrained by the SRAM access latency of 0.67 ns, and CCSS operates at 1.5 GHz accordingly. The total power consumption remains below 40W.
\textbf{Compilation}: Benchmarks are synthesized via Yosys~\cite{wolf2013yosys}, producing LUT-based netlists compiled into CCSS instructions using Python and C++. These instructions drive the architecture simulator for performance evaluation.
\subsection{Hardware Features} In CCSS, each LUT has an instruction depth of 512. With 5 LUTs per core, one core simulates 2560 LUTs. The two-level 36×36 core topology totals 1296 cores, enabling simulation of 3.31 million LUTs and the area is 449.36$ \mathrm{mm}^2 $ given by Design Compiler. Consequently, \textbf{CCSS} attains an emulation‐capacity density of
$0.135\,\text{mm}^2/\text{kLUT4}$.  Commercial FPGAs fabricated in the
same process node provide roughly
$0.28\,\text{mm}^2/\text{kLUT6}$.  According to a Xilinx report, one
\textit{LUT6} can replace approximately $1.6$ \textit{LUT4}; therefore,
the FPGA figure converts to 0.28 / 1.6 $\approx$ 0.175 \text{mm$^2$}/\text{kLUT4}.
Hence, CCSS delivers a capacity‐density improvement of (0.175 - 0.135) / 0.175 $\approx$ 23\% over FPGAs at the same technology node.


\subsection{Benchmark}
\textbf{VTA}: Versatile Tensor Accelerator — a flexible, open-source deep learning accelerator~\cite{moreau2019hardware}. \textbf{RV32R}: A 16 in-order, pipelined RISC-V processor implemented in Chisel~\cite{kim2023riscvmini}. \textbf{MC}: A Monte Carlo-based FPGA engine for stock option pricing~\cite{tian2008design}.

\subsection{Baseline}

In the RTL simulation acceleration experiment, our work addresses the slow performance of functional-debug simulations by targeting single-stimulus, long-cycle workloads commonly encountered in real-world designs, and we thus compare our approach primarily against conventional CPU-based simulators and multi-core DSA specialized for simulation acceleration. We use Verilator~\cite{verilator2020} and Manticore~\cite{emami2023manticore} as baselines. Verilator is an efficient open-source tool that converts Verilog to C++ for faster simulation, supporting both single- and multi-threaded modes on CPUs. Manticore is an multi-core DSA that speeds up simulation through parallelism, offering higher efficiency.
