\textbf{Instruction analysis.} 
By conducting instruction analysis in Figure~\ref{fig:prop}, we find the acceleration achieved by CCSS can mainly be attributed to small proportion of idle instructions. As idle instructions arise mainly from load imbalance, this observation indicates that our fiber-based graph partition algorithm effectively achieves load balancing across cores. As a result of few idle and synchronization instructions, the simulation speedup of CCSS can be roughly estimated to be proportional to the number of LUTs, showing the computational scalability of our design.


\section{Discussion and Future Work}
\label{sec:futurework}

CCSS offers a viable approach for RTL acceleration. While the current SRAM design is functionally correct, it introduces notable area and power overhead due to architectural complexity. We also explored constructing multi-port memory using DFFs in Verilog, but under the TSMC 7nm process, the critical path reached 10.3 ns, limiting frequency to below 100 MHz and rendering it impractical. Although we did not conduct FPGA-based experiments, this choice was made to align with the Manticore benchmark suite, where all test cases fit on a single FPGA. In such scenarios, FPGAs can achieve higher frequencies than CCSS but incur significantly longer compilation times, which our design avoids. We therefore focus on evaluating end-to-end efficiency under a consistent benchmark setting. Achieving the high energy efficiency targeted by CCSS will require custom-designed multi-port memory blocks, which we leave for future work.
\section{Conclusion}

This paper presents CCSS, an LUT-based accelerator for full-cycle RTL simulation that combines balanced DAG partitioning with deeply pipelined compute cores to accelerate combinational logic, and integrates a low-latency on-chip network to streamline sequential synchronization. Evaluated on benchmark designs, CCSS achieves up to 12.9× speedup over state-of-the-art multi-core simulators, demonstrating the effectiveness of its co-designed hardware and compilation flow.
