Running QEMU to simulate a RISC-V 32I ISA on the PS (ARM Cortex-A9 dual-core processor) introduces both hardware and software overhead. The QEMU and the RISC-V-related dependencies (libglib2.0, glibc, etc.) \cite{qemu} require approximately 650MB in the storage. At runtime, QEMU consumes around 48.8\% of the DDR3 (~ 250MB/512MB). The biggest performance impact comes from QEMU’s dynamic binary translation (DBT), introducing a translation overhead due to the single-threaded nature of QEMU's TCG (Tiny Code Generator), which heavily loads one ARM core while leaving the second core underutilized.



