\subsection{Related Work}

% Recent works about non-intrusive verification were trying to leverage the readback technology for FPGA-assisted emulation platforms. %Angepat et al. introduced 
% NIFD \cite{NIFD} is a framework that facilitates single-step execution through a debugging interface with a bandwidth of 500 KB/s. While this approach mirrors the functionality of a GNU debugger, it proves inadequate for verifying many concurrent signals in complex RTL designs. GNOSIS \cite{gnosis} employs readback technology for automated hardware execution verification and enables VCD file dumping for debugging purposes. However, it lacks support for the readback of FPGA on-chip memory. The StateMover \cite{statemover_2020} was proposed as an FPGA-based verification platform with a debugging bandwidth of 100 MB/s. However, this framework necessitates intricate configurations of the data paths specific to individual tasks and substantial hardware utilization, consuming up to 40K programmable slices on Xilinx Ultrascale KCU105. 

% Moreover, all the above-mentioned methods apply a direct debugging interface between FPGAs and the host machines. In contrast, FERIVer offloads the primary validation tasks from the host CPU by managing the critical communication between the RTL DUT and the reference model through a high-bandwidth channel (145 MB/s, Section \hyperref[performance]{4.2}) between the PS and PL on the FPGA SoC, significantly enhancing verification efficiency.

Recent works on non-intrusive verification have explored readback technology for FPGA-assisted emulation platforms. NIFD \cite{NIFD} facilitates single-step execution through a debugging interface with a bandwidth of 500 KB/s; however, it is insufficient for verifying many concurrent signals in complex RTL designs. GNOSIS \cite{gnosis} automates hardware execution verification and enables VCD file dumping for debugging but lacks support for FPGA on-chip memory readback. StateMover \cite{statemover_2020} achieves a debugging bandwidth of 100 MB/s but requires intricate configurations that adapt to specific data paths of different tasks. Besides, the substantial hardware utilization, 40K slices on Xilinx Ultrascale KCU105, is another limitation for further scaling. In contrast, FERIVer overcomes these limitations by offloading the primary validation tasks from the host CPU and managing critical communication between the RTL DUT and the reference model via a high-bandwidth channel (140 MB/s, \hyperref[performance]{\S 4.2}) between the PS and PL on the FPGA SoC, significantly enhancing verification efficiency while minimizing resource overhead (0.6K slices on Xilinx Zynq XC7Z020, \hyperref[sec-evaluation]{\S 4.1}).


\section{ Conclusion and Outlook} \label{sec-conclusion}
% Introducing FERIVer, a unique FPGA-assisted verification framework that stands out for its cycle-accurate verification of RISC-V processors. FERIVer's efficiency is further enhanced by using both hard IP processors and soft IP cores on AMD Xilinx SoC. We also present a dynamic and agile mechanism that allows FERIVer to parse checkpoints of the executions of RISC-V instructions. This mechanism can be easily applied to verify RISC-V or other computer architectures by simply replacing the abstract model and the RTL model under test. The evaluation results clearly demonstrate FERIVer's capability to substantially save time and effort in verifying complex processor designs at a very low cost. The lightweight nature of the open-source tools used in FERIVer also contributes to cheaper and faster verification, enhancing the flexibility of design space exploration and streamlining the overall design process. Our ongoing task focuses on implementing dedicated RISC-V ISA extensions and multi-core systems for high-performance computation scenarios.

This work introduced FERIVer, an FPGA-assisted verification framework that is capable of cycle-accurate verification of RISC-V processors. It exhibits high efficiency by employing both hard IP processors and soft IP cores on AMD Xilinx SoC. Furthermore, we presented a dynamic and agile mechanism to parse checkpoints of the executions of the instructions, which can be applied to verify RISC-V or other computer architectures by simply replacing the abstract model and the RTL model under test. The evaluation results demonstrated the capability of significant time and effort savings for the verification of complex processor designs at very low costs. Our ongoing task focuses on optimizing the data throughput from PL to PS and verifying dedicated RISC-V ISA extensions for high-performance computation scenarios.

