%The growing dominance of linear state space models (LSSMs) in long sequence prediction tasks, stems from their ability to speed up learning, through a parallel scan over the state and input transition matrices. This dramatically overshadowed nonlinear, state space models (NSSMs), whose learning was sequential. In early versions, the state-transition matrix of LSSMs was constrained to be diagonal and constant. Today, LSSMs improved their accuracy, by making this matrix input dependent at every timestep. However, this improvement still fails to capture important correlations among the input elements, typically summarized by the state. In this paper, we show how state dependence can also be achieved, for liquid-resistance liquid-capacitance neural networks (LRCs), a bioinspired NSSM (a nonlinear RNN). Our approach, which we call LrcSSM, learns a nonlinear, diagonal, state-and-input-dependent state-transition matrix, which is efficiently discretized at every timestep for use in the parallel scan. Our experiments demonstrate that LrcSSM networks outperform state-of-the-art LSSMs, including LRU, S5, and Mamba, particularly in capturing long-range correlations. Our work also opens the way towards parallelization of bioinspired architectures. Finally, our work seems to be easily generalizable to other nonlinear biological or artificial NSSMs, too. 

