\paragraph{Caching}
Initializing a Lean REPL is expensive because it must load large libraries such as \texttt{Mathlib}. To avoid paying that cost for every request, the server reuses workers that already contain the right set of imports. Scripts are split into a \texttt{header} and a \texttt{body}, and workers are indexed by their header in an LRU cache. When a new script arrives, the server grabs a warmed worker with the same header (or creates one if needed) and verifies only the body. This reuse of ready contexts, combined with parallel execution, delivers large speed-ups. 

