\subsection{Related Work}

\subsubsection{Deep Models for Time Series Forecasting}
Time series forecasting, a cornerstone of time series analysis, has garnered extensive attention in both research and application domains. Deep learning models for this task can be categorized into five major paradigms: RNN-based, CNN-based, Transformer-based, MLP-based, and Diffusion-based approaches. RNN-based models~\cite{salinas2020deepar,du2021adarnn}, unlike traditional methods, avoid presupposing specific temporal structures. Their recurrent architecture facilitates capturing nonlinear and complex dependencies by modeling intermediate state transitions. However, the sequential nature of RNNs often leads to error propagation over long time horizons. CNN-based models~\cite{wang2023micn,franceschi2019unsupervised,liu2022scinet} excel at extracting local patterns through convolutional kernels, but their predictive capacity is typically limited by a constrained receptive field. Transformer-based models~\cite{zhou2021informer,liu2023itransformer,chen2021autoformer} leverage the multi-head self-attention mechanism to effectively capture long-range dependencies, achieving state-of-the-art performance in forecasting tasks. Despite these advantages, their quadratic time and memory complexity pose scalability challenges. MLP-based models~\cite{zeng2023transformers,yi2024frequency,wang2024timemixer} offer a simpler architecture, modeling relationships between input and output sequences across both time and frequency domains, and have demonstrated promising results in recent studies. Diffusion-based models~\cite{shen2023non,yuan2024diffusion,alcaraz2022diffusion} harness the generative and denoising capabilities of diffusion processes to deliver high-quality time series prediction and generation. This categorization underscores the diverse methodological advancements in deep learning for time series forecasting.

\subsubsection{Diffusion Models for Time Series Forecasting}

TimeGrad, the pioneering diffusion model for time series forecasting~\cite{rasul2021autoregressive}, integrates a Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM)~\cite{ho2020denoising} with a recurrent neural network (RNN) to encode historical data. While this approach marks a significant innovation, its reliance on autoregressive decoding introduces challenges such as error accumulation and slower inference speeds. Building on this foundation, CSDI~\cite{tashiro2021csdi} enhances the diffusion framework by incorporating self-supervised masking, which effectively guides the denoising process. TimeDiff~\cite{shen2023non} takes a non-autoregressive approach, further advancing diffusion models to deliver high-quality time series forecasting without the drawbacks of autoregressive methods. Diffusion-TS~\cite{yuan2024diffusion} employs a Transformer as its backbone, leveraging time series decomposition to separately generate trend and seasonal components within the decoder. This architecture is complemented by a loss function that integrates both time-domain and frequency-domain information, enabling more accurate and robust predictions.

\subsubsection{Related Generative Models}
Generative modeling has advanced significantly in the last decade, driven by seminal works like generative adversarial nets~\cite{goodfellow2014generativeadversarialnetworks}, variational auto-encoders~\cite{doersch2021tutorialvariationalautoencoders}, and normalizing flows~\cite{rezende2016variationalinferencenormalizingflows}. These frameworks laid the foundation for modeling complex data distributions through adversarial training, variational inference, or invertible transformations. More recently, score matching~\cite{song2021scorebasedgenerativemodelingstochastic} and diffusion models~\cite{ho2020denoising} emerged as powerful alternatives, augmenting VAEs hierarchically while restricting distributions to Gaussian assumptions. However, their reliance on discrete timesteps introduced computational and theoretical complexities, akin to classic discrete normalizing flows. Flow Matching~\cite{lipman2023flowmatchinggenerativemodeling} addresses these challenges by formulating a continuous-time ordinary differential equation (ODE) that connects a source distribution to a target distribution. By learning a velocity field that guides samples along this trajectory, Flow Matching enables efficient sampling and likelihood estimation. Several interpolation mechanisms have been introduced to learn this velocity field, such as Rectified Flow Matching~\cite{albergo2023buildingnormalizingflowsstochastic,liu2022flowstraightfastlearning} and Stochastic Interpolants~\cite{albergo2023stochasticinterpolantsunifyingframework}, which linearly interpolate between source and target samples. These approaches promote straight flow trajectories, simplifying ODE solving while demonstrating impressive scalability and performance on large-scale datasets~\cite{esser2024scalingrectifiedflowtransformers}. Theoretical foundations for continuous-time modeling in generative frameworks are rooted in Neural ODEs~\cite{chen2019neuralordinarydifferentialequations}, which enable parameter-efficient and adaptive computation. Recent advancements in flow matching have extended these principles to diverse domains, including text generation~\cite{DBLP:conf/eacl/HuWAMFOS24}, time series~\cite{kollovieh2024flowmatchinggaussianprocess,TFM}. 


