\begin{example}\label{ex:discoord}
The SNC game on the network (a) in Figure \ref{fig:disc_game} with external field $h=0$ reduces to a two-player game with binary action set $\mc A=\{\pm1\}$ and utilities $u_1(x_1, x_2) = x_1x_2$ and $u_2(x_1, x_2) =-x_1x_2$.  This is commonly referred to as the discoordination game \cite{jackson2015games} and known not to possess Nash equilibria. 
\end{example} 
\begin{example}\label{ex:3ringanticoord}
The SNC game with binary actions on the network (b) in Figure \ref{fig:disc_game} with external field $h=0$ is an anti-coordination game. For $x^*$ to be a Nash equilibrium, one should have $x_1^*=-x_2^*=x_3^*=-x_1^*$, which is impossible in $\{\pm1\}^3$. Hence, this game does not admit any Nash equilibria.
\end{example} 


Even for SNC games that do admit Nash equilibria, these may fail to be  globally BR-reachable or BR-invariant, as illustrated in the following two examples. 

\begin{example}\label{ex:onelink} Consider a SNC game on a network $\mc G=(\mc V,\mc E,W)$ with two nodes  $\mc V=\{1,2\}$, a single link $\mc E=\{(1,2)\}$, and weight matrix $W=(1,0)'(0,1)\,,$ 
and  external field $h=(0,\alpha)$, where $\alpha$ is a scalar parameter. Then, the set of Nash equilibria is 
$$\mc N=\left\{\ba{lcl}
\{-\1\}&\se&\alpha<0\,,\\
\{\pm\1\}&\se&\alpha=0\,,\\
\{\1\}&\se& \alpha>0\,.
\ea\right.$$
It is easily verifiable that $\mc N$ is globally BR-reachable for every $\alpha$. 
In fact, for $\alpha\ne0$ then the unique Nash equilibrium is strict, so that $\mc N$ is BR-invariant, hence globally BR-stable. On the other hand, for $\alpha=0$, there are two Nash equilibria neither of which is strict: in this case, neither $\mc N$ nor any of it subsets are BR-reachable, and there are no globally BR-stable sets. 
\end{example}



