\subsection{Summary of Contributions}
This paper focuses on the maximal achievable rate for each individual object, denoted \( \lambda_j^{\max} \), and establishes a connection between the SRR problem, MLD, and combinatorial design theory. Concretely, the main contributions of this work are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We establish upper and lower bounds on the maximal achievable demand for each data object in a system encoded by a generator matrix \( \boldsymbol{G} \), expressed in terms of the number of parity checks orthogonal to its minimal recovery set and the parameters of the underlying code. Unlike previously established bounds that apply only to specific codes (e.g., Reed--Muller codes) or code families (e.g., systematic codes), our result holds for all binary linear codes (Theorem~\ref{thm:general_bound_demand}).

    \item We specialize these bounds to the case where \( \boldsymbol{G} \) is systematic and show that the upper bound is achieved when the minimum-weight dual codewords satisfy a specific structural condition. This reveals a direct connection between the maximal achievable demand in the SRR problem and the error-correction capability of MLD applied to the underlying code (Theorem~\ref{thm:systematic} and Corollary~\ref{coro:2desgin}).
    \item We verify that the bounds are tight for all codes whose SRR has previously been characterized in the literature (Remark~\ref{remark:verify} and Section~\ref{sec:Systematic}.B).
    \item We extend the analysis to Hamming codes, providing the first characterization of their service rate region (Theorem~\ref{thm:Hamming_demand}). 
\end{enumerate}

